[node name="ReadmeText" type="Label" parent="UI/ReadmePanel/ScrollContainer" unique_id=1236644402]
custom_minimum_size = Vector2(1420, 1600)
layout_mode = 2
text = "MORNING DRIVE

Student Name: Yaroslav Shelest

Project Overview

Morning Drive is a short interactive Godot experience about a simple morning routine that turns into a small driving challenge. The player begins in a mini-market, collects groceries, pays at the cashier, exits to the street, enters a car, and then plays a 2D driving mini-game to get home.

The project combines 3D exploration, interaction zones, UI dialogue, item selection, a budget system, cashier interaction, scene transitions, and a 2D obstacle-avoidance driving section.

Response to the Brief

The assignment asked for an interactive experience based on a day in the life. I interpreted this as a morning shopping trip before driving home. Instead of making only one scene, I created a flow where the player moves through several everyday actions: entering a shop, choosing products, paying, leaving the shop, finding the car, and driving home.

Controls

3D Sections:
- WASD - Move
- Mouse - Look around
- E - Interact
- 1 / 2 / 3 - Choose products from shelves
- 1 / 2 - Choose dialogue options

2D Driving Mini-Game:
- A / D or Left / Right - Change lanes
- Start Button - Begin driving section

Gameplay Structure

1. README / Start Screen
The player starts with an information screen explaining the project and controls.

2. Shop Scene
The player explores a 3D mini-market and can approach shelves to view products.

3. Basket Interaction
The player must pick up a basket before buying products.

4. Product Selection
Different shelves offer different products such as cereal, milk, sugar, flour, apples, water, juice, and bread.

5. Budget System
At the start of the shop scene, the player receives a random budget. The basket total updates as products are collected.

6. Cashier Interaction
The player talks to the cashier to complete the purchase. If the basket total is higher than the budget, the cashier suggests putting back the last selected item.

7. Street Scene
After checkout, the player exits to the street and approaches the car.

8. Car Interaction
The player can choose to go home. A car engine sound plays before the game transitions into the 2D mini-game.

9. 2D Driving Mini-Game
The player drives home while avoiding obstacles. The player has three lives shown as hearts.

10. Ending
If the player survives until the progress bar reaches the end, the game displays a success message. If all lives are lost, the game displays Game Over.

Implemented Features

- 3D shop environment
- First-person player movement
- Mouse-look camera control
- Interactive shelf zones
- Product selection UI
- Basket requirement system
- Random budget system
- Basket total tracking
- Cashier dialogue system
- Over-budget item removal logic
- Scene transition from shop to street
- 3D street scene
- Car interaction zone
- Engine start sound before transition
- 2D driving mini-game
- Lane-switching car movement
- Random obstacle spawning
- Three-life heart system
- Obstacle collision system
- Progress bar for reaching home
- Background music in the mini-game
- Crash sound on collision
- Game Over state
- Win state

Visual and Aesthetic Direction

The project uses a simple low-poly 3D style for the shop and street scenes, using Kenney asset packs for the environment, shelves, products, buildings, roads, nature, and vehicles. The 2D mini-game uses a pixel-art style with a top-down road, car sprite, hearts, progress bar, and obstacle sprites.

The contrast between the 3D shopping section and the 2D driving mini-game gives the project a small but complete game structure.

Assets and Credits

3D Assets:
- Kenney Mini Market Kit
- Kenney Car Kit
- Kenney City/Suburban Kit
- Kenney Road Kit
- Kenney Nature Kit

2D Assets:
- Car sprite
- Heart sprite
- Cone obstacle sprite
- Broken cone sprite

Music and Sound:
- Background driving music: Alphaville - Big In Japan
- Car starter / engine sound: https://zvukipro.com/transport/589-zvuki-zavodjaschejsja-i-ne-zavodjaschejsja-mashiny.html
- Crash sound: 
-Shop background: A View To A Kill by Duran Duran 

What I Created Myself

- Overall game concept and structure
- Shop scene layout
- Street scene layout
- Interaction logic
- Shelf product system
- Basket system
- Budget and cashier logic
- Scene transitions
- 2D mini-game structure
- Obstacle spawning
- Health/lives system
- UI layout and gameplay flow
- Custom obstacle behaviour
- Code integration between scenes

Development Reflection

The most challenging part of the project was connecting several different systems together. The shop scene needed product selection, basket logic, a budget system, and cashier dialogue. It was difficult to make these systems work together without confusing the player.

Another challenge was connecting the 3D section to the 2D mini-game. I had to manage scene transitions, mouse control, UI visibility, and music/sound behaviour. The 2D mini-game also required testing lane positions, obstacle spawning, collision, lives, and progress timing.

This project helped me understand Godot’s node structure, scenes, scripts, Area3D interaction zones, UI control nodes, timers, audio players, and scene transitions.

Future Improvements

- Add more products and shelf variety
- Add more cashier dialogue
- Add more obstacle types in the driving mini-game
- Add better win and lose screens
- Improve the 2D scaling for different screen resolutions
- Add more sound effects
- Add more animations for the cashier and car
- Improve the visual polish of the street scene

Links

Itch.io page: https://shelest-kva.itch.io
GitHub repository: https://github.com/shelestyaroslav05/Assignment
Video demo: https://www.youtube.com/@ЯрославШелест-и2ъ/videos

Computational Elements

The project uses Godot’s node and scene system, Area3D interaction zones, UI panels, input handling, timers, random values, scene transitions, audio playback, object spawning, collision detection, and state-based gameplay logic.

Aesthetic Elements

The project combines a low-poly 3D shopping/street environment with a pixel-art 2D driving mini-game. The simple visual style makes the experience readable and accessible while still showing a complete game flow.

Documentation

This README explains the concept, controls, gameplay structure, implemented features, assets, personal contribution, development reflection, future improvements, and project links.

